In this lesson we'll look at how the area of a face can lead you straight to the volume of a whole prism. Here's the big idea. Imagine slicing a right prism into a stack of super thin layers, each one a perfect copy of the same cross-section. Every layer has the same area, call it A. Stack up as many layers as the height of the prism, and you just get area times height. That's it. Same formula, two faces. For a rectangular prism, the cross-section is a rectangle, so its area is length times width, and the volume is that multiplied by the height of the prism. For a triangular prism, the cross-section is a triangle, so its area is half the base times the triangle's own height, and you multiply that by the height of the whole prism. Quick note on those two different heights. The little triangle has its own perpendicular height, and the whole prism has its own perpendicular height. They are different numbers in general, so don't mix them up. Now let's see it in action.